35,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"It's worse than I thought. Our yards use a mix of old gear, and a previous trial failed because people just went back to spreadsheets. Plus, trying to cut over during peak season was a disaster.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"My findings confirm a worrying trend. The board easily approves recurring operating costs while cutting capital requests, and separately licensed add-ons have seen yearly price hikes. We must avoid a modular pricing trap.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"My findings are equally concerning. Last winter, a critical outage went unowned for two days because support was scattered across four suppliers. Furthermore, our project scopes are too rigid to survive a single year of business changes.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Rin,,I knew it! The dispatch tool is a total dinosaur—they've skipped three upgrades because they're too busy to test it. And that 2018 data move was a mess; external people left us with errors for a whole year!,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings reveal a fragile foundation. We are losing our internal knowledge to retirement, and new hires are scarce. Moreover, standard training is often ignored in favor of old habits at the depots.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Asuka, if we have a mix of old gear and a history of failing trials, we can't afford a rigid system. We need something that actually fits the yard's reality, not just a fancy manual.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Kenji, I agree. Flexibility is important, but we must be careful. If we choose a system with many separate add-ons to get that flexibility, we risk the price creeping up every year without the board noticing.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Mizuki, that is a valid point. However, we also cannot afford a fragmented support structure. If we have multiple vendors for different modules, we risk another outage where no one takes ownership of the problem.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Asuka, that's exactly what happened with the data move! External people did the work and then we were stuck with the mess. We need someone who actually stays and fixes things, not just a ""partner"" who leaves!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It seems we are all describing a house built on sand. We lack the staff to maintain complex systems, and our people on the ground resist rigid, external impositions. We need a partner who provides stability and genuine support.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Servas, you're right. If the depot crews just drop the training and go back to their own ways, we need a vendor who actually understands our environment and stays on-site to make it work.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Kenji, that sounds like a capital-heavy approach. Given the board's tendency to trim capital requests but ignore operating costs, we must ensure the pricing model is transparent and doesn't hide long-term costs in separate, climbing licenses.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Mizuki, transparency is essential, but not at the cost of accountability. We need a single point of contact for support to avoid the finger-pointing we saw last winter, regardless of whether the cost is capital or operating.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Rin,,"@Asuka, exactly! If we have a system that just ages in place because it's too hard to upgrade, we're doomed. We need a vendor who handles the hard stuff and doesn't just leave us with a broken mess!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"We must seek a path that respects both the budget and the human spirit. A solution that is too rigid will be rejected by the depots, while one too fragmented will break our weary administrators.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"I'm convinced we need a vendor who doesn't just sell us software, but actually helps us implement it on the floor and sticks around to make sure it survives our peak season.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,I agree. We need a solution that balances operational support with a predictable cost structure. A bundled or hosted model might be safer than separate licenses that can quietly inflate over time.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"I agree. To avoid the failures of the past, we must prioritize a vendor that offers a unified support structure and a flexible approach to scope, ensuring we aren't left without ownership when things break.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Rin,,"I'm with you all! We need a vendor who won't just give us a ""dinosaur"" that we can't upgrade and who won't leave us cleaning up their data messes for a year!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,"We have spoken truthfully of our fears. We need a partner who bridges the gap between the office and the yard, providing a steady hand to guide our remaining staff through this transition.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
